---
// THE TRIFECTA DECK (founder 2026-08-07; rewritten to the founder's own edit
// 2026-08-08) — one page for Andrea Pomarico (Four Winds Wholeness Sanctuary)
// and Steve Dobo (The Audacity Lab).
//
// THE COPY IS THE FOUNDER'S, VERBATIM (2026-08-08: "Make the literal edits I
// input verbatim. Where I leave notes in ALL CAPS, I want you to take a stab
// at adding the copy. If I deleted copy, delete it on the page, verbatim.").
// Sections Claude drafted to the founder's ALL-CAPS notes, to be reviewed:
// "A Community Hub, in action", Audacity Lab's second benefit, "Holistic
// healing", and "Owner financing changes the math". Deleted per the same
// edit: "Separately, each of these...", three of the platform bullets
// (community / programme-group / KAHUBA front door), "Somewhere to start",
// and "The shape of it".
//
// ⚠ FINANCIAL CONTENT: the $4.8M figure and the open-market financing
// comparison are on the page BY THE FOUNDER'S EXPLICIT DIRECTION (the
// owner-financing ALL-CAPS note). Andrea's own terms remain absent — she adds
// those herself. Nothing else states a term, instrument or return.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WeavePitch from '../../components/WeavePitch.astro';
import FourWindsPhones from '../../components/FourWindsPhones.astro';
import TangoScene from '../../components/TangoScene.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Four Winds · Audacity Lab · Lichen"
  description="Three organisations, forming a perfect pilot partnership: land held in trust, young people growing up alongside it, and the infrastructure that makes both repeatable."
>
  <WeavePitch partner="Four Winds & Audacity Lab">
    <Fragment slot="ecosystem">
      <FourWindsPhones />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="economy">
      <TangoScene kids />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="weave">
      <span class="eyebrow">Three organisations, forming a perfect pilot partnership</span>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What each of us brings</h3>

      <h4 class="display worg">Four Winds</h4>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>The land.</strong> Thirty-five acres against the
          Front Range, between Pikes Peak and Longs Peak. The 1899 Wolfensberger
          barn, whose acoustics are said to rival Carnegie Hall. Pastures,
          ponds, ancient pines and dark skies. Horses, llamas, goats and a wild
          mustang, alongside the deer, elk, fox and owls that were already here.
          A regenerative farm with agricultural status, an organic garden and
          greenhouse, lodging and practitioner housing, and fifteen years of
          programming for veterans, first responders, trauma survivors, people
          with special needs, and anyone else who came to grow. It is also
          KAHUBA&rsquo;s headquarters — seventeen years of sanctuary work, 45+
          rescued horses, llamas, goats, dogs and feral cats given a forever
          home, and long-standing partnerships with Mt. Carmel Veterans Center,
          Wild Hearts Haven, The Imagine Project, H.O.O.V.E.S. and Purple Lotus
          WARRIOR Yoga. This becomes the first property held in a
          purpose-formed land trust.
        </li>
        <li>
          {/* Founder's copy, verbatim (2026-08-08 tweak). */}
          <strong>A Community Hub, in action.</strong> Long before she had a
          tech platform to help her, Andrea built the gathering place: concerts
          and ceremonies in the historic barn, workshops and retreats,
          community events and holistic fairs, high-schoolers earning service
          hours alongside the animals, partner nonprofits sharing the venue,
          and farm-to-table meals feeding all of it. Four Winds doesn&rsquo;t
          need to become a Community hub — it needs its years of already being
          one to be tracked, counted and curated with greater ease, thanks to
          Silicon based infrastructure.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h4 class="display worg">Audacity Lab</h4>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Education.</strong> Steve&rsquo;s work
          brings dreamers together to prototype the future one experiment at a
          time — Montessori microschools, the mobile classroom, and reach into
          communities that conscious-media platforms never touch. Steve is also
          a former Four Winds board president, which means he isn&rsquo;t
          arriving with a hypothesis about this land. He already knows what it
          can hold. Audacity Lab becomes the education and next-generation arm
          on the ground: young people growing up alongside land, animals and a
          working farm, learning stewardship and reciprocity by living them
          rather than reading about them.
        </li>
        <li>
          {/* Founder's copy, verbatim (2026-08-08 tweak). */}
          <strong>A web of partners.</strong> Audacity Lab is an incubator of
          organizations innovating for the better future that Lichen network
          members participate in, collectively. Zero Dropouts, Up With Africa,
          and portfolio organizations like African Montessori Hub — training
          Montessori educators from Harare, with its Denver partner Montessori
          on Wheels — mean the education arm lands with communities on two
          continents, ready to plug in and collaborate more effectively on the
          Lichen platform. Nonprofit collaboration opens donor doors a platform
          alone can&rsquo;t, and Steve&rsquo;s standing in Colorado education
          anchors the pilot in local credibility.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h4 class="display worg">Lichen</h4>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          {/* STRUCK, NOT DELETED (founder 2026-08-08): Lichen's scope is the
              technology partnership. The strikethrough shows a seat
              deliberately left open; the slot invites the partner who fills
              it. Matches the scope email. */}
          <s class="wstrike"><strong>Holistic healing.</strong> Lichen&rsquo;s care model — a
          Concierge team forming around one person, human wisdom and AI
          coordinating the next, best step — needs a place where every layer of
          healing is within arm&rsquo;s reach. Four Winds is that place,
          already: fifteen years of programming for veterans, first responders
          and trauma survivors; equine therapy infrastructure and animals who
          are themselves practitioners; practitioner housing, quiet, and food
          grown on the same ground where care is given. Galyn has run an
          integrated clinical, equine and land-based healing centre before —
          Four Winds is where that model gets piloted on land that is ready for
          it: care teams that can include a therapist, a horse, a garden and
          the place itself, every contribution counted.</s>
          <span class="wslot">⟨ insert partner here ⟩</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technology infrastructure.</strong> The technology layer that
          weaves it all together. The
          Current-cy economy that records every contribution once it&rsquo;s
          stewarded — hay, work-trade, animal care, the hours a teenager puts
          into the garden, the people who simply show up. And the network every
          contributor is woven into, rather than left holding a receipt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <s class="wstrike"><strong>Business &amp; legal infrastructure.</strong> The
          land-trust structure that receives what&rsquo;s given and holds the
          ground, and the business layer that runs the property day to day.</s>
          <span class="wslot">⟨ insert partner here ⟩</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Together, these 3 organizations create an entire ecosystem</h3>

      <h4 class="display worg">4 Winds</h4>
      <p class="wbody">
        Andrea has created a fully functional, community center for healing,
        growth, education and community commerce, in reciprocal relationship to
        the plants, animals, land and spirits at 4 winds. By initiating a
        purpose-formed land trust with the 4 Winds property, she is pioneering a new
        way of facilitating land ownership and stewardship, which can be
        replicated by like-hearted land owners around the world. The land
        can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the programming, the programming has
        somewhere permanent to stand, and the non-human contributions that make
        4 winds a magical place to learn, grow, heal and connect are counted
        and compensated, in reciprocity.
      </p>

      <ul class="wexamples">
        <li>
          <strong>Four Winds becomes a place.</strong> Every room and thing it
          stewards goes onto a real calendar — the barn, the arena, the therapy
          rooms, the guest house, the stables, the truck, the long tables. What
          the sanctuary already has stops sitting idle, and anyone booking it
          does so through a steward who approves each request. The land itself
          is a member, not a backdrop: a beyond-human entity whose contributions
          are recorded, and whose care is funded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab becomes an Organization.</strong> Its Lichen page
          is a real website on the open web — programmes, offerings, a calendar,
          bookable sessions, and a team — updating itself as the work changes,
          because it draws from the same listings members already see. Donors,
          schools and families find it without a separate site to maintain.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Castle Rock becomes a geo-focused Community &amp; Concierge
          healing hub.</strong> Because Andrea has created a working economy
          that supports the land, animals and people of Castle Rock; with the
          addition of Lichen&rsquo;s Healing programming and Audacity
          Lab&rsquo;s educational offerings, 4 Winds is the perfect, pilot
          Community hub on the Lichen network.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Young people are not left behind.</strong> With their own
          contributions recorded in an economy that counts an afternoon of
          fence-mending and an hour of teaching as the real things they are. A
          young person who grows up inside the Lichen ecosystem leaves with a
          ledger of what they gave, and a web of people who know it, as well as
          the opportunity to give and receive value within a new social order.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What Four Winds proves</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Four Winds is uniquely ready to be first — healed, turnkey, legally and
        agriculturally established. It is the pilot: the place where Lichen&rsquo;s
        model stops being an idea and becomes an address people can visit,
        measure and participate in. Audacity Lab is a partner in that pilot, not
        a tenant of it. What the education arm learns here — how young people
        change when they grow up alongside land held in common — is part of what
        the pilot is for, and it travels to every property that follows.
      </p>

      <p class="wcallout">
        The land can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the work. That is the whole
        point, and it is the one promise no ordinary sale can make.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The economy is already running there</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Walking through the operating history, the striking thing isn&rsquo;t
        the costs. It&rsquo;s how much of Four Winds already runs on exactly
        what Lichen was built to count. Land care happens through work-trade. A
        neighbour farms the fields in exchange for half the crop. The vet
        donates her services because she loves what the education centre does.
        Students get paid to stack hay. Llama fibre and pellets hold the
        agricultural status. Volunteers arrive, and some of them stay.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        None of that appears in any ledger. It is a reciprocity economy already
        operating in the analog — held together by Andrea&rsquo;s relationships
        and fifteen years of goodwill, and invisible the moment she steps back.
        The gap isn&rsquo;t generosity; Four Winds has never lacked that. The
        gap is that nobody has ever <em>counted</em> it, which means it
        can&rsquo;t be seen by a funder, scaled to another property, or handed
        to a successor. That is precisely the gap Current-cy closes: every hour,
        every bale, every trade recorded and returned to the person who gave it.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        And what the land actually needs turns out to be smaller and more
        concrete than the conversation usually assumes: a resourced steward.
        Someone living there whose job is the place.
      </p>

      {/* Drafted to the founder's ALL-CAPS note (cost-effectiveness of Andrea
          owner-financing the sale; honoring her restoration and mentorship).
          The conventional-financing math is stated with its assumption named;
          Andrea's own terms are deliberately absent — she adds those. */}
      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Owner financing changes the math</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        There is a conventional way to buy a $4.8 million property: borrow it.
        On a thirty-year note near seven percent, that purchase costs roughly
        $11.5 million by the time it&rsquo;s paid — close to $7 million of it
        interest, flowing to lenders who will never set foot on the land, feed
        an animal, or teach a child to muck a stall.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        Andrea is offering the other way: she finances the sale herself, taking
        her equity out over time. Her terms are hers to state, and she&rsquo;ll
        add them to this deck — but the shape of the difference is already
        clear. The margin a bank would have taken stays in the ecosystem, the
        land is never collateral in someone else&rsquo;s portfolio, and the
        payments go to the person who spent fifteen years restoring the ground
        being bought.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        Paid out over time, Andrea isn&rsquo;t just the seller — she&rsquo;s the
        bridge: compensated for the restoration the land now embodies, and
        present through the handover as mentor to the stewards who come next.
        The structure honors her twice — once for what she built, and once for
        what she still has to teach.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </WeavePitch>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .h2-weave { font-size: clamp(24px, 3.5vw, 36px); margin: 4px 0 0; }
  .worg {
    font-size: var(--t-xl); font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
  }
  /* A struck seat: still legible — the point is showing what was deliberately
     left open — but clearly not Lichen's to claim. */
  .wstrike { text-decoration: line-through; text-decoration-thickness: 1.5px; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .wstrike strong { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  /* The open seat itself: a dashed slot waiting for a name. */
  .wslot {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: 6px;
    padding: 2px 12px; border: 1.5px dashed var(--peach);
    border-radius: var(--r-full);
    font-style: italic; font-size: var(--t-sm); color: var(--peach-deep);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
